The first crack ran through the east wing when Elias blundered the bishop, and plaster sifted down like dead snow from the chandeliers. In the mirror, his final opponent stood where no reflection should be, pale hands folded over a face that never aged beyond the last move. He had come home disgraced, but the manor had been crumbling long before the papers called him ruined; now it seemed to answer each lapse in him with masonry, rot, and bloodless thunder.

from a worse position. He had lost his queen close to a thousand times before he earned his grandmaster title,

Yet tonight every mistake was different. A bad retreat in the kitchen and the floor tilted, pouring ink-black water through the tiles. A greedy sacrifice and the nursery door vanished into brick. He understood, with a terror more intimate than prayer, that he was still playing, and that the board was his life, and that somewhere ahead his own final checkmate waited to collapse the house around him.
